#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""agy — bridge CLI between local agents (Claude Code & friends) and Google Gemini.

TWO LANES, on purpose: they coexist, they do not compete.

  1. agentapi lane — drives the live Antigravity app: an AUTONOMOUS agent with a
     terminal, a workspace and its own permission model (`new`, `send`, `models`, and
     the read commands that watch it).
  2. Gemini API lane — talks straight to generativelanguage.googleapis.com for
     INFERENCE (`ask`, `img`, `tts`, `embed`, `video`, `models --api`). No daemon, no app,
     no workspace: one HTTP call, an answer, done. This is the default for "I need
     a model to think", the agentapi lane is for "I need an agent to act".

Surfaces used (the Antigravity app's own, plus the public Gemini API):
  * agentapi        — official CLI subcommand of the language_server daemon (write path)
  * ~/.gemini/antigravity/brain/<conv>/          — artifacts + transcripts (read path)
  * ~/.gemini/antigravity/conversations/*.db     — SQLite step ledger (read path, ro)
  * generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta     — the public Gemini API (lane 2)
  * the daemon's own TLS port (Connect protocol) — read-only RPCs for `quota`, `models`,
    the post-dispatch model check, `show` and `ledger`, plus DeleteCascadeTrajectory for
    `gc --yes`. This is the ONE internal surface here, called with the same CSRF token
    and a hard timeout, and ONLY for the methods in RPC_ALLOWLIST (everything else,
    DeleteAllUserData included, is refused before a socket opens). An app update can
    rename an RPC — and then the command says exactly that instead of pretending.

Security invariants (enforced here, documented in SKILL.md):
  * Disk surfaces are READ-ONLY. All writes go through agentapi.
  * The CSRF token is read fresh from the live process args on every run and is
    NEVER printed, logged or persisted. `agy status` only reports its presence.
  * The Gemini API key gets the SAME treatment: read fresh per run (env override,
    then Keychain), never printed/logged/persisted, and it travels ONLY in the
    `x-goog-api-key` HEADER — never as `?key=`, which would leak the secret into
    proxy logs, shell history and any error message that echoes the URL.
  * Every call — agentapi and HTTP alike — has a hard timeout, so nothing hangs.
  * `new`/`send` (autonomous agent + quota) and `video` (real money per clip)
    require --yes.

No cache by design: the daemon's gRPC port and CSRF token live and die together
with the process, so we rediscover both on every invocation (<0.5s). No cache,
no staleness class, no "stale token vs denied access" ambiguity.

Exit codes:
  0  ok
  1  general / CLI / catalog validation error
  2  daemon not running or unreachable
  3  authentication / CSRF token error
  4  model mismatch (dispatched, but ran a different model than requested)
  5  dispatched, but the model that ran could not be verified
"""

